## Layout

The code follows domain-driven design in a ports-and-adapters layout: the
domain is at the center, the application layer runs the use cases on it, and
every piece of input and output is an adapter around them.

| Directory | Contents |
|---|---|
| `{{.Slots.entry.Dir}}` | Entry point: configuration and wiring of every layer |
| `{{.Slots.models.Dir}}` | Domain: the `Item` entity, the rules for item names, and the `ItemStore` port |
| `{{.Slots.app.Dir}}` | Application: `ItemService`, which runs the item use cases and checks the domain rules |
| `{{.Slots.handlers.Dir}}` | HTTP adapter: the router, the items handlers, and `/health` |
| `{{.Slots.store.Dir}}` | Persistence adapter: data access, implementing `ItemStore` |
| `{{.Slots.database.Dir}}` | Database adapter: connection and schema, used by the persistence adapter |
{{- if .Slots.web.Filled}}
| `{{.Slots.web.Dir}}` | Web adapter: the web pages and their static files, served at `/` |
{{- end}}

Dependencies point inward: the adapters and `{{.Slots.app.Package}}` import
`{{.Slots.models.Package}}` and use or implement its ports, the adapters never
import `{{.Slots.app.Package}}`, and `{{.Slots.models.Package}}`
imports no other package of the project. Only `{{.Slots.entry.Dir}}` knows
every package and connects them: the HTTP adapter
{{- if .Slots.web.Filled}} and the web adapter get{{else}} gets{{end}} the application
service, which stores items through the persistence adapter.

The HTTP adapter
{{- if .Slots.web.Filled}} and the web adapter depend{{else}} depends{{end}} only on the `{{.Slots.models.Package}}.ItemStore`
port, which the application service implements. When the use cases grow
beyond storing and listing, give the handlers an interface of their own with
the service's methods. The handlers encode `{{.Slots.models.Package}}.Item`
directly; when the API and the domain diverge, add response types to
`{{.Slots.handlers.Dir}}`.

### Adding an aggregate

To add, for example, orders next to items:

1. Add `order.go` to `{{.Slots.models.Dir}}` with the `Order` entity, its
   rules, and an `OrderStore` port. Once the domain holds several aggregates,
   give each one a package of its own, such as `{{.Slots.models.Dir}}/order`.
2. Add an `OrderService` to `{{.Slots.app.Dir}}` for the order use cases. If
   they need items, give the service the `ItemStore` port, never an adapter.
3. Implement `OrderStore` in `{{.Slots.store.Dir}}`, following the items
   store, and add its tables to the schema in `{{.Slots.database.Dir}}`.
4. Add the order handlers to `{{.Slots.handlers.Dir}}` and their routes to
   `NewRouter`, and in `{{.Slots.entry.Dir}}/main.go` create the store and the
   service and pass the service to `newHandler`, which gives it to the router.
